return [
    18 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Business Planning for Providers',
                'summary' => 'Run the provider as a business.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Accreditation opens the door, but a business plan is what keeps a provider profitable behind it. Business planning for providers turns a trading operation into a plan with targets and margins.\n\nStart with the numbers you can control: your cost of goods, your operating costs, and your price. The margin between them is the business, and the provider who knows their margin knows what to sell, what to push, and what to drop.\n\nSet a few targets. A plan with five targets has none; a plan with two, expressed in monthly sales, margin, and new customers, focuses the whole business. Write them down, because the target that is written is the target that is worked.\n\nPlan the cash, not just the sales. Stock must be bought before it is sold, and the gap between the two is where providers run out of money. A cash plan that shows the gap lets you fund it deliberately instead of by surprise.\n\nFinally, review the plan monthly against the actuals. The provider who treats the plan as a living document steers the business; the provider who files it steers by luck, and luck is not an accreditation standard.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Service for Providers',
                'summary' => 'Service that earns repeat buyers.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "For a provider, customer service is the product as much as the stock. Customer service for providers is the discipline of making every interaction build the trust that accreditation promises.\n\nStart with responsiveness. Inquiries answered within a day, and orders confirmed clearly, are the baseline. The provider who replies fast signals reliability, and reliability is what a buyer is really buying from a verified provider.\n\nGet the order right, every time. The right product, the right quantity, the right condition, on time. Service failures are the main reason buyers leave a provider, and most of them are prevented by a simple order-checking routine before dispatch.\n\nHandle the problem well. A short delivery or a damaged item, owned and fixed quickly, becomes a story the buyer tells in the provider's favour. The provider who recovers well turns a mistake into loyalty, and the provider who argues turns it into a bad review.\n\nFinally, ask for feedback and act on it. The provider who listens to buyers learns what the market wants and where the service slips. Service that learns is service that keeps the badge and the buyers.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Logistics and Delivery Operations',
                'summary' => 'Deliver right, on time, every time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A provider's promise is only as good as its delivery. Logistics and delivery operations is the discipline of moving stock to the customer intact, on time, and at a cost the margin can carry.\n\nStart with the dispatch routine. Pick, check, pack, label: a fixed routine applied to every order is what makes delivery reliable. The order checked twice at dispatch arrives right; the order rushed out arrives wrong, and the wrong arrival costs twice.\n\nChoose the delivery method for the goods. Fragile, heavy, perishable, or high-value goods each need their own handling and transport. Matching the method to the goods protects both the product and the margin, and the buyer who receives intact goods returns.\n\nManage the cost. Delivery is a real cost that belongs in the price, not a surprise for the customer or the provider. Know the cost per delivery, per zone, and price it, because the provider who hides delivery cost in the margin slowly loses it.\n\nFinally, track and confirm. A delivery tracked to the customer, and confirmed received, closes the loop. The provider whose deliveries are tracked and confirmed is the provider the marketplace can trust, and trust is the accredited provider's currency.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Digital Skills for Providers',
                'summary' => 'Sell well on the digital shelf.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Accreditation is delivered and sold on a digital platform, and a provider's digital skills now decide how far the business reaches. Digital skills for providers is the discipline of using the platform's tools fluently.\n\nStart with the storefront. A complete profile, accurate listings, and clear photos are the digital equivalent of a clean, well-signposted shop. The buyer who can see what they are buying buys with confidence, and confidence converts.\n\nLearn the platform's flows: how an order is placed, how payment is protected, and how a dispute is raised. A provider who understands the flow can guide a buyer through it, resolve issues quickly, and avoid the mistakes that trigger disputes.\n\nUse the messaging and reporting tools. Prompt, clear communication through the platform keeps the transaction protected and the record clean. The provider who moves the conversation onto the platform protects both the buyer and themselves.\n\nFinally, read the platform's numbers: views, inquiries, conversion, and ratings. The provider who reads these and adjusts is growing on the digital shelf; the provider who ignores them is invisible in a crowded market. Digital fluency is now a core business skill.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Growing a Multi-Region Operation',
                'summary' => 'Scale beyond your home base.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Accreditation in one region is a foundation; a multi-region operation is the growth built on it. Growing a multi-region operation is the discipline of expanding without breaking the service that earned the badge.\n\nStart with the reason to expand. A new region should have real demand you can serve, not just ambition. The provider who expands into a region with buyers ready, stock that travels, and a delivery plan is the provider who grows profitably; the one who expands on hope grows thin.\n\nReplicate the standard, not just the brand. What made you accredited, the verification, the service, the quality, must travel with you. The provider who keeps the standard in every region keeps the trust; the one who lets it slip in a new region loses the badge everywhere.\n\nBuild the local capability. A region you cannot serve yourself needs a trusted local partner, agent, or delivery arrangement, vetted as carefully as your own operation. Multi-region growth is a chain, and it is only as strong as the weakest link.\n\nFinally, grow at the speed your systems can carry. The provider who expands faster than their stock, delivery, and support can handle is the provider whose ratings fall. Growth that is paced by capability is growth that lasts.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    19 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Mapping Your Territory',
                'summary' => 'Know the ground you cover.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A certified field agent works a territory, and the agent who knows that territory covers it efficiently and completely. Mapping your territory is the discipline of knowing the ground, the growers, and the gaps.\n\nStart with the map of your area: the villages, the farms, the access roads, and the terrain. A physical or digital map, marked with what you know, turns a vague region into a set of places you can plan to reach.\n\nLayer the growers onto it. Who farms where, who is already registered, and who is eligible but not yet enrolled. The map that shows the enrolled against the eligible shows your work clearly: the gap areas are your priority.\n\nPlan the routes. Group visits by proximity so a day covers ground rather than distance. The agent who plans routes from the map sees more farms in a week than the agent who drives on instinct, and the saved travel time becomes verification time.\n\nFinally, keep the map current. New farms, new access, new growers: the map that is updated is the map that stays useful. The agent who maintains their own map is the agent who truly owns their territory, and coverage that is mapped is coverage that is complete.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Grower Retention and Engagement',
                'summary' => 'Keep growers active in the program.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Enrolling a grower is the start; keeping them active is the program's real measure. Grower retention and engagement is the discipline of keeping growers participating, year after year.\n\nStart with the first weeks. A newly enrolled grower who is contacted, welcomed, and shown what the program offers in their first month stays; one who is enrolled and ignored quietly drops out. The agent who follows up early keeps the grower.\n\nKeep the value visible. Remind growers, at the right season, what the program gives them: inputs, market access, verification, or support. A grower who sees the value stays; a grower who forgets it drifts. Your visit is the reminder that keeps the value in mind.\n\nListen to the friction. A grower who complains about a process, a delay, or a benefit is telling you where the program loses people. The agent who reports that friction, and the program that fixes it, keeps the growers the friction would have driven away.\n\nFinally, measure the retention. Enrollment that is not retained is enrollment that failed. The agent who tracks which growers stay active, and works to keep them, is the agent whose program endures rather than merely starts.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data Quality Audits',
                'summary' => 'Audit the records the program runs on.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The program runs on its data, and data quality audits are how a field agent keeps that data trustworthy. A record that cannot be trusted cannot drive a decision, a payment, or a verification.\n\nStart with the audit question: does this record prove what it claims? An enrollment record that names a grower, places them at a verifiable location, and links them to their farm is a record that can be trusted; one missing any of these is a record to be fixed.\n\nSample, do not skim. Review a sample of records against the standard, including the ones that look fine, because the errors that matter hide in the ordinary. The auditor who samples honestly finds the pattern; the one who only checks the obvious finds nothing.\n\nFix at the source. A data error is usually a field error: a rushed GPS, an unchecked name, a missing photo. The fix is in the field, at the moment of capture, not in the office. The agent who audits and then fixes the capture improves the data at its source.\n\nFinally, report the pattern, not just the error. A repeated error points at a process or a training gap. The agent who reports the pattern helps the whole program improve, and data quality that is audited is data the program can build on.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Supporting Program Campaigns',
                'summary' => 'Run the campaign in the field.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Programs run in campaigns: enrollment drives, verification pushes, and seasonal efforts. Supporting program campaigns is the discipline of executing the campaign in the field, where the plan meets the farms.\n\nStart by understanding the campaign's goal and its measure. An enrollment drive wants a certain number of eligible growers, verified to standard; a verification push wants a certain number of farms confirmed. The agent who knows the target knows what success looks like and can pace their days to reach it.\n\nPrepare before you go. Materials, a charged device, a mapped route, and a list of target farms turn a campaign day into a productive one. The agent who prepares turns the campaign's ambition into completed visits.\n\nWork the standard under pressure. Campaigns are busy, and the temptation is to rush the verification to hit the number. The agent who keeps the standard under pressure is the agent whose campaign results survive audit; the numbers that fail the audit are not results.\n\nFinally, report daily during the campaign. The campaign office needs the day's numbers to steer the effort. The agent who reports daily is the agent whose field reality shapes the campaign, and a campaign that is steered by real data reaches its target.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Professional Development Plan',
                'summary' => 'Grow your career on purpose.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A certified field agent has a career, and the agents who advance are the ones who plan it. Professional development is the discipline of growing your skills and your standing on purpose, not by chance.\n\nStart with where you are and where you want to be. The next role, the next skill, the next certification: name it, because a career that is named is a career that can be planned, and a career left to drift goes wherever the current takes it.\n\nBuild the skills the next role needs. Supervisory roles need leadership and reporting; specialist roles need deeper technical skill. The agent who studies for the next role, in the quiet hours, arrives prepared when the opportunity opens.\n\nCollect the evidence. Your completed verifications, your data quality, your feedback, and your campaign results are the record of your capability. The agent who keeps this evidence can show it when it matters, and evidence, not hope, wins the promotion.\n\nFinally, find the mentors and the feedback. Ask the senior agents and coordinators how you are doing, and act on the answer. The agent who seeks feedback shortens the climb, and a career that is planned and coached is a career that reaches its destination.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    21 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Product Development and Range',
                'summary' => 'Build a range buyers want.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A certified seller's growth is built on the range, not just the listing. Product development and range is the discipline of offering products that buyers want, in the grades and forms they will pay for.\n\nStart with the buyer. What do your best customers actually buy, and what do they ask for that you do not sell? The range grows from real demand, and the seller who listens to inquiries and reviews grows a range that sells itself.\n\nDevelop the grades and forms. The same produce, cleaned, graded, packed, or processed into a related product, reaches different buyers at different prices. The seller who adds a grade or a form adds margin without adding a new product from scratch.\n\nTest before you commit. A small trial batch of a new product tells you whether buyers want it before you stock a warehouse of it. The seller who tests cheaply and scales what works is the seller who grows without gambling.\n\nFinally, prune the range. Products that do not sell tie up stock and money and crowd the store. The certified seller who keeps a tight, high-quality range, and rotates it with the seasons, is the seller whose store buyers trust and return to.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Packaging and Presentation',
                'summary' => 'Present the product as its worth.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Buyers judge the product before they taste or use it, and packaging and presentation is what shapes that judgement. Presentation is not vanity; it is the signal of quality and care that justifies the price.\n\nStart with protection. Packaging's first job is to get the product to the buyer intact. The pack that survives transport, keeps the product clean, and prevents damage is the pack that protects the sale and the rating, because an damaged arrival is a refund and a bad review.\n\nMake it clear and honest. Label the product, the grade, the weight, and the origin. A buyer who can read exactly what they are buying buys with confidence, and confidence is what converts a browser into a buyer and a buyer into a repeat customer.\n\nMake it look the part. Clean, consistent, and professional presentation, even a simple tidy pack, signals quality. The seller whose product looks the part can ask the price that quality deserves, and the buyer who receives a well-presented product trusts the seller next time.\n\nFinally, watch the packaging cost. Premium packaging earns its keep only if the price it supports exceeds its cost. The certified seller who balances protection, clarity, appeal, and cost presents the product at its true worth.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Relationship Management',
                'summary' => 'Turn buyers into regular customers.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A sale is the start of a relationship, not the end. Customer relationship management is the discipline of turning one-time buyers into regular customers whose repeat business steadies the store.\n\nStart with the record. A simple note of each buyer, what they bought, and when they last ordered is the foundation. The seller who remembers a returning customer's preference, or follows up after a first order, is the seller whose customers feel known, and customers who feel known come back.\n\nKeep in touch, usefully. A message about a new product in the range, or the return of a seasonal favourite, is welcome; a stream of spam is not. The seller who communicates usefully stays in the customer's mind without becoming a nuisance.\n\nReward the loyal customer. A repeat buyer deserves a better price, an early look, or a priority service, because retaining a customer costs far less than winning a new one. The seller who rewards loyalty builds a floor of steady demand beneath the market's ups and downs.\n\nFinally, recover the lapsed customer. A buyer who has not ordered in a while is worth a polite check-in. The certified seller who manages the whole relationship, from first sale to lapsed customer, builds a business with a steady heartbeat.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Competitive Analysis',
                'summary' => 'Know the market you sell in.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "No seller operates alone, and the sellers who win are the ones who know their market. Competitive analysis is the discipline of watching the competitors, the prices, and the trends, and adjusting before the market moves you.\n\nStart with the competitor set. Who sells the same or similar products, at what price, with what ratings and delivery? The seller who knows the competitor's offer knows where their own price and service stand, and can position against it deliberately.\n\nWatch the price and the gap. Price is the most visible competition, but rarely the only one. The seller who competes on quality, reliability, or range, rather than only on price, protects their margin and their business, because the price war is the war nobody wins.\n\nRead the trends in the market. What is rising in demand, what is falling, and what new product is appearing? The seller who spots the trend early captures it; the seller who spots it late competes for the scraps.\n\nFinally, act on what you learn. A competitor's price cut, a new product, or a service gap is a signal to respond. The certified seller who analyses the market and then acts, rather than merely watches, grows a business that leads rather than follows.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Exit, Sale and Succession',
                'summary' => 'Build a business with an end in mind.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every business eventually changes hands, by sale, by succession, or by closing. Exit, sale and succession is the discipline of building a business that can be valued and passed on, so the value you built is not lost.\n\nStart with the records that make a business sellable. Clean financials, a documented customer base, a reliable supply, and a store that runs on process rather than on the owner's presence: these are what a buyer pays for, and the seller who builds them builds a business with a price.\n\nValue the business honestly. A business is worth its sustainable profit, its assets, and its customer base, not its potential. The seller who knows the real value does not give the business away, and does not overprice it into a sale that never happens.\n\nPlan the succession early. Whether the business passes to a family member, a manager, or a buyer, the transition works when it is planned, rehearsed, and gradual, not when it is forced by an event. The seller who plans the handover protects the employees and the customers too.\n\nFinally, keep the platform reputation clean, because a store with strong ratings and a clean record is the asset a buyer wants. The certified seller who builds for the exit, from the beginning, builds a business that outlasts them.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    134 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Managing the State Budget',
                'summary' => 'Stretch every naira of program money.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A state coordinator is a manager of money as much as of people, and the state budget is the tool that turns program funds into results. Managing the state budget is the discipline of spending deliberately and accounting for every naira.\n\nStart with the plan before the money. What does the state intend to deliver, and what will each activity cost? A budget built from the plan is a budget that funds the mission; a budget that exists only to absorb funds is a budget that wastes them.\n\nTrack against the budget, monthly. Actual spending against the planned, line by line, is what keeps the program solvent. The coordinator who watches the variance monthly catches the overspend while there is time to correct it, rather than at the end when it is too late.\n\nSpend on the priorities. The budget is a statement of the state's priorities, so the money should flow to the activities that matter most, not to the loudest requests. The coordinator who defends the priorities in the budget defends the mission in the field.\n\nFinally, keep the evidence. Every spend needs its record: the invoice, the approval, and the result it bought. The coordinator who accounts for the money cleanly keeps the program's audit clean, and a clean audit is what funds the next year's work.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Recruitment and Team Building',
                'summary' => 'Build the state\'s field team.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A state program is delivered by its people, and the coordinator who builds the right team delivers more than the one who inherits one. Recruitment and team building is the discipline of getting the right people and making them a team.\n\nStart with the role, not the resume. Define what the job actually requires, the skills, the geography, and the temperament, and hire against that definition. The agent hired for the right role, with the right habits, performs; the one hired on a hunch is a cost.\n\nBuild for character and train for skill. Reliability, honesty, and the willingness to work in the field are harder to teach than the technical steps. The coordinator who hires for character and trains for skill builds a team that stays and improves.\n\nMake the team, not just the group. A team shares the standard, communicates, and covers for each other; a group is just people in the same place. The coordinator who builds the shared standard and the communication habit turns a group of agents into a team.\n\nFinally, grow the leaders. Identify the agents who show judgment and develop them, because a state that grows its own leaders is a state that never depends on a single person. The coordinator who builds leaders builds a program that outlasts them.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Cross-State Collaboration',
                'summary' => 'Work with other states to win.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "States do not operate in isolation, and the coordinator who collaborates across states gets more done than the one who competes with them. Cross-state collaboration is the discipline of sharing what works and solving what is shared.\n\nStart with what is common. The pests, the markets, the suppliers, and the program standards are often shared across states. The problems that are shared are the problems best solved together, and the coordinator who sees the shared problem sees the collaboration.\n\nShare the lessons. A fix discovered in one state is a fix available to all, if it is shared. The coordinator who shares a working practice, and the coordinator who adopts a practice from elsewhere, both make the whole program stronger for the same effort.\n\nCoordinate on the boundaries. Farms and markets do not respect state lines, so verification, supply, and disputes often cross them. The coordinators who agree how to handle the boundary cases solve them in a day; the ones who do not, solve them in a month of friction.\n\nFinally, build the relationships. Collaboration runs on trust between coordinators, and trust is built by keeping commitments. The coordinator who collaborates well, and keeps their word, builds a network that makes their own state's work easier and the program's whole greater than the sum of its states.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Advocacy and Government Relations',
                'summary' => 'Win the environment the program needs.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A state program lives inside a government, and the coordinator who works with that government well wins the support the program needs. Advocacy and government relations is the discipline of building that support deliberately.\n\nStart with the value, stated plainly. What does the program deliver for the state's farmers and its economy, in numbers the official can use? The program that can show its value in the government's own language gets the official's support, because every official needs results to show.\n\nKnow the decision-makers and their priorities. Each level of government has its own goals, and the coordinator who understands them can show how the program serves them. Advocacy is not asking for help; it is showing how the program helps the official's own agenda.\n\nKeep the relationship current, not transactional. Regular reporting, honest problems, and shared credit build the relationship that survives a difficult season. The coordinator who only appears when they need something burns the relationship, and a burnt relationship is a lost ally.\n\nFinally, deliver and report. The strongest advocacy is a program that visibly works. The coordinator who delivers results and reports them clearly builds the reputation that opens the doors, and the doors that are opened by results stay open.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Legacy and Succession Planning',
                'summary' => 'Leave a state that runs without you.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The measure of a coordinator is what the state looks like after they move on. Legacy and succession planning is the discipline of leaving a state that runs on its own systems, not on your presence.\n\nStart by documenting how things work. The processes, the contacts, the arrangements, and the standards, written down, are the state's memory. The state that is documented survives a coordinator's departure; the state that lives in one person's head collapses with it.\n\nBuild the deputy. Identify the person who will lead next, and develop them deliberately: the decisions, the relationships, the hard conversations. The coordinator who grows their own successor leaves a state that does not miss a beat, and a successor who is ready is a program that continues.\n\nSpread the knowledge, not just the power. A state that depends on one person is a state at risk; a state where several people can lead is a state that endures. The coordinator who trains broadly and shares the judgment builds resilience, and resilience is the real legacy.\n\nFinally, leave it better. The systems improved, the people developed, and the results delivered are what remain. The coordinator who plans their legacy leaves a state that runs, grows, and outlasts them, and that is the highest mark of the operations program.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    149 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Nursery Business Management',
                'summary' => 'Run the nursery as a business.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A nursery produces seedlings, but a nursery business produces profit. Nursery business management is the discipline of running the nursery as a business with costs, margins, and a plan, not just a patch of seedlings.\n\nStart with the cost per seedling. Every input, the seed nuts, the media, the water, the labour, the shade, and the overhead, divided by the seedlings produced, gives the true cost. The nursery that knows its cost per seedling knows the price it must charge and the margin it earns.\n\nPlan the production cycle. Seedlings take months, and the nursery that plans its sowing so seedlings are ready when buyers want them sells at the best price, while the nursery that is unprepared sells late and cheap. The calendar is the nursery's plan.\n\nPrice for the market and the quality. Graded, certified, healthy seedlings command a better price, and the nursery that grades honestly and prices to the quality earns the margin that keeps it improving. The price that covers cost and improvement is the price that sustains the nursery.\n\nFinally, keep the records and review them. Sales, costs, germination rates, and losses, recorded and reviewed each cycle, are what turn a nursery that survives into a nursery business that grows. The manager who reads the numbers manages the business, not just the seedlings.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Variety Selection and Improvement',
                'summary' => 'Grow the right variety, better.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The variety a nursery produces decides what the farmer's farm will be for decades. Variety selection and improvement is the discipline of choosing and refining the varieties that best serve the farm and the market.\n\nStart with the end use. Different varieties suit different climates, soils, and markets, and the buyer's needs. The nursery that selects the variety for the farm's conditions and the market's demand gives the farmer a palm that thrives; the nursery that sells one variety everywhere gives the farmer a gamble.\n\nSource the parent stock carefully. The quality of the seedling is capped by the quality of the mother palms and the seed nuts. The nursery that sources from selected, high-yielding, healthy palms produces seedlings with a better genetic start, and that start is what the farmer pays for.\n\nImprove by selection. Within a variety, the best palms produce the best seed nuts, and selecting the parents is how a nursery improves its stock over time. The nursery that selects deliberately, and records which palms produced the best seedlings, is a nursery that gets better each generation.\n\nFinally, keep the variety honest. Label the variety accurately, and do not pass a common seedling off as a premium variety. The nursery that is honest about its varieties builds the trust that accreditation is built on, and trust is the nursery's most valuable stock.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Water Management in the Nursery',
                'summary' => 'Water seedlings for steady growth.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Seedlings are small, shallow-rooted, and vulnerable to water stress, so nursery water management is the discipline of giving them steady, even moisture without waterlogging them.\n\nStart with the medium. The media's job is to hold moisture and drain excess, so a good nursery medium holds water like a sponge and lets the surplus run. Media that stays soggy drowns the roots, and media that dries too fast starves the seedling, so the medium is the first water decision.\n\nWater evenly and regularly. A seedling that swings between soaked and dry is a seedling that grows unevenly. The nursery that waters to keep the medium evenly moist, adjusting for heat, wind, and rain, grows the uniform seedlings that buyers pay for.\n\nWatch the signs. Wilting, yellowing, and stunted growth are the seedlings' signals, and each points at a different water problem. The nursery manager who reads the seedlings and adjusts the watering catches the problem early, while the manager who waters on a fixed schedule misses it until the batch is lost.\n\nFinally, plan for your absence. Seedlings need water every day, including the days you are not there. The nursery with a watering plan, a schedule, and a person who owns it is the nursery that survives the busy week and the holiday, and consistent water is the seedling's most basic need.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Media Preparation and Composting',
                'summary' => 'Build the soil the seedling grows in.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The growing medium is the seedling's whole world for its first months, and media preparation and composting is the discipline of building a medium that feeds and supports it.\n\nStart with the ingredients. A good nursery medium combines a structure, like coir or sand, with organic matter and, where possible, compost. Each ingredient contributes drainage, water-holding, or nutrition, and the medium that balances all three grows the strongest seedlings.\n\nCompost properly. Compost is the cheapest source of organic matter and slow nutrition, but it must be made well. A compost pile that heats, is turned, and is finished before use feeds the seedlings; a pile that is half-rotted burns them and carries disease. The nursery that composts correctly turns waste into its best input.\n\nSterilise or screen the medium. Soil-borne pests and diseases live in reused media, and the nursery that treats or screens its medium, or buys clean inputs, avoids the losses that sterilisation prevents. The cost of clean media is less than the cost of a diseased batch.\n\nFinally, test and adjust. The medium's texture and, where possible, its pH and nutrition, should be checked and corrected. The nursery that treats its medium as a recipe, recorded and refined each batch, is the nursery whose seedlings consistently outperform, and the medium is the recipe's foundation.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Service and Seedling Sales',
                'summary' => 'Serve the farmer who buys your seedlings.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A nursery's reputation is built on the farmer it serves, and customer service and seedling sales is the discipline of making that service as strong as the seedlings themselves.\n\nStart with honest advice. The farmer who asks which variety, how many, and when to plant deserves a straight answer, matched to their land and their market. The nursery that advises honestly, even when it sells fewer seedlings, builds the trust that brings the farmer back every season.\n\nGrade and label clearly. A seedling sold with its variety, its age, and its grade clearly marked is a seedling the farmer can plan with. The nursery that grades and labels honestly sells confidence, and confidence is what a farmer is really buying alongside the seedling.\n\nHandle the seedling well at sale. Seedlings are living, and the way they are packed, watered, and handed over decides how they arrive. The nursery that treats the seedling carefully at the point of sale protects the farmer's first weeks, and the farmer whose seedlings arrive strong returns.\n\nFinally, follow up. A nursery that checks the seedling took, and advises through the first season, is a nursery the farmer relies on. The service that follows the sale is the service that turns a seedling purchase into a multi-year relationship, and relationships are the nursery's steady business.",
            ],
        ],
    ],
];
